---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { loadManifest } from '@data/loadSnapshot';
import { formatDate } from '@data/derive';
import {
  ABOUT_UPDATED,
  CONTACT_EMAIL,
  GITHUB_REPO_URL,
  OPERATOR_NAME,
} from '@data/site';

const manifest = loadManifest();
const { ruleset } = manifest;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — WoW Forever Talent Calculator"
  description="About this independent WoW Forever talent planner: who it is for, where the data comes from, how evidence is labeled, corrections, and how the site is funded."
  dateModified={ABOUT_UPDATED}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'About', url: '/about/' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <h1>About This Site</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      WoW Forever Talent Calculator is an independent, fan-made planning tool.
      It is free to use, requires no account, and runs on openly licensed
      data with every uncertain value labeled as such.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The site is maintained by the {OPERATOR_NAME}, a small fan project. Its
      source code is public on
      {' '}<a href={GITHUB_REPO_URL} rel="external">GitHub</a>, and you can
      reach us through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>
      WoW Forever brings back a talent system that players plan carefully:
      three trees per class, a limited pool of points, and choices that are
      costly to undo. Before launch, the information players need is scattered
      across demo footage, beta screenshots and spreadsheets, and it is hard
      to tell which of it is current. We built this site to put that
      information in one place, let people try builds against the real
      rules, and be explicit about how much is actually known.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is for players who want to plan a character before they level
      it, for guild officers and friends who want to send each other a build
      that opens exactly as intended, and for anyone who simply wants to read
      what a talent does without hunting through forum posts. It is not aimed
      at theorycrafters who need simulation, and it does not pretend to be.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="what-it-does">
    <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
    <p>
      The site covers all nine classes with three talent trees each. On the
      <a href="/">calculator</a> you spend points under the standard rules:
      a budget that depends on level, row gates that open every
      {ruleset.pointsPerRowTier} points spent in the rows above, and
      prerequisites between talents. The level range is
      {' '}{ruleset.minLevel} to {ruleset.maxLevel}, and a level
      {' '}{ruleset.maxLevel} character has {ruleset.maxBudget} points.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Share links.</strong> A build becomes a link that contains
        only the class, level, data snapshot and ranks. Opening one shows the
        build read-only, with an option to edit a copy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Saved builds.</strong> Builds are saved in your browser on
        your own device, with rename, delete, export and import on the
        <a href="/my-builds/">My Builds</a> page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Comparison.</strong> The <a href="/compare/">Compare</a> page
        shows exactly which talents differ between two builds of the same
        class and data snapshot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reference pages.</strong> The
        <a href="/talents/">talent reference</a> lists every talent, rank text,
        evidence status and Classic comparison as plain HTML, and the
        <a href="/changes/">Changes page</a> counts what is new, changed,
        moved or removed against Classic.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a step-by-step tour, read the
      <a href="/blog/how-to-use-the-wow-forever-talent-calculator/">calculator guide</a>
      on our blog.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="what-it-does-not">
    <h2 id="what-it-does-not">What it deliberately does not do</h2>
    <p>
      This is a planner, not a simulator. There is no damage simulation, no
      gear modeling and no automatic "best build" recommendation, and we do
      not publish tier lists. Where the data is uncertain, the site says so
      for that specific rank instead of filling gaps with guesses or quietly
      borrowing Classic values. The standard budget also leaves out Legacy
      early-spend effects; if a separate budget mode is confirmed, it would be
      added as its own profile instead of changing the standard one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="data">
    <h2 id="data">Data, license and attribution</h2>
    <p>
      Talent data comes from
      {' '}<a href="https://talentsforever.com/" rel="external">talentsforever.com</a>,
      which publishes its export under
      {' '}<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="external">CC BY 4.0</a>.
      We credit it on the calculator and on the <a href="/sources/">Sources
      page</a>, which also lists the source records, the coverage summary and
      the update policy. We reformat and annotate the data, for example by
      attaching an evidence status to each rank, and say so where it
      matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CC BY 4.0 license applies to the data export. It does not change who
      owns the game. World of Warcraft, its names, talent text and icons
      belong to Blizzard Entertainment. The talent icons on this site are
      Blizzard game assets, bundled with the site to help players identify
      talents; we claim no ownership of them. If a rights holder asks us to
      remove material, we review the request and act on it, and
      <a href="/contact/">the contact page</a> says how to send one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="handling">
    <h2 id="handling">How the data is handled</h2>
    <p>
      We keep the raw export in the repository and run an import that turns
      it into dated, content-hashed snapshots, one file per class. The
      current snapshot was published on {formatDate(manifest.publishedAt)}
      {manifest.gameBuild ? <> and reflects beta build {manifest.gameBuild}</> : null}.
      When a new build's data is available upstream, we publish a new snapshot
      and record the differences in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
      Older share links keep working: renamed talents keep their points,
      points in removed talents are refunded, and a note on the page says
      what changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every rank has its own evidence status, from "official" down to
      "unknown". Right now {manifest.coverage.ranksCommunityRecorded} of
      {' '}{manifest.coverage.ranksTotal} ranks carry the community-record
      label, which means we pass along what the source reports and have not
      checked each value against the game client ourselves. That is why the
      snapshot is marked "{manifest.stage}" and why you will not see a rank
      described as confirmed unless it is. The
      <a href="/blog/where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from/">data origin post</a>
      explains the labels and the limits in more depth.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="editorial">
    <h2 id="editorial">Editorial principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Say what we know and how.</strong> Uncertain values are
        labeled, not hidden or rounded into certainty.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Do not invent mechanics.</strong> Articles on the
        <a href="/blog/">blog</a> are written from the dataset or from public
        announcements, and talent sections are generated from the snapshot so
        they stay in step with the reference pages.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep the record.</strong> Data changes go in the changelog,
        and posts show an updated date when we correct them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep money out of the data.</strong> Advertising does not
        decide what a talent says, which talents are shown, or what we write.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="funding">
    <h2 id="funding">How the site is funded</h2>
    <p>
      The site shows advertising through Google AdSense, and that revenue
      supports the work of running and maintaining the site. Ads are served
      by Google, not by us, and the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>
      describes the cookies and choices that come with them. The site has no
      paywall and no account system.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="contact">
    <h2 id="contact">Corrections &amp; contact</h2>
    <p>
      Found a wrong rank text, a broken prerequisite, a stale date or a bug?
      Email
      {' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
      {' '}or open an issue on the project's
      {' '}<a href={`${GITHUB_REPO_URL}/issues`} rel="external">GitHub tracker</a>.
      Include the class, talent name and rank, and the snapshot ID shown on
      the <a href="/sources/">Sources page</a>, so we can check the report
      against the exact data you saw. We read every report, but we run the
      site in spare time and do not promise a reply time. The
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> lists what to include for each
      kind of message.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="whats-next">
    <h2 id="whats-next">What is confirmed to come</h2>
    <p>
      We only list what is already public. Blizzard has announced the global
      launch of WoW Forever for November 4, 2026, at 3:00 p.m. PST; the
      <a href="/wow-forever-beta-end/">beta end and launch dates</a> page
      keeps the full schedule. The site will continue to follow published
      beta data until then and record each update in the changelog. Beyond
      that, we do not make commitments about features or dates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section" aria-labelledby="faq">
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Is this an official Blizzard site?</summary>
      <p>
        No. It is an independent fan project with no affiliation to
        Blizzard Entertainment and no endorsement from it.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Do I need an account?</summary>
      <p>
        No. There is no sign-up or login. Saved builds stay in your browser
        and share links carry only the build.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Can I trust the talent descriptions?</summary>
      <p>
        Treat them as a planning aid. Each rank shows its evidence status, and
        for choices that matter you should compare the text with the game.
        Corrections are welcome.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Who writes the blog?</summary>
      <p>
        Posts are published under the site's name by the {OPERATOR_NAME}, and
        we do not use invented author profiles.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Can I reuse the data?</summary>
      <p>
        The upstream export is CC BY 4.0, so follow its terms and credit
        talentsforever.com. Game names, text and icons remain Blizzard's.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section prose" aria-labelledby="independence">
    <h2 id="independence">Independence notice</h2>
    <p>
      Independent fan-made tool. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Blizzard
      Entertainment. Game names and related terms belong to their respective
      owners.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
